-- Type synonym cycles that a name can hide (SHOULD FAIL).
-- Each of these synonyms refers to itself, but not by the spelling a reader
-- would look for. They used to be accepted, and unifying two of them (as
-- oops does below) used all the memory there was.
DECLARE Duo OF a, b
  HAS fst IS AN a
      snd IS A  b

-- `B1` here has no arguments, so it is W1's own alias, not the synonym
-- `B1 a` below, which takes one: W1 is `Duo OF W1, W1`.
DECLARE W1 AKA B1 IS A Duo OF B1, B1
DECLARE B1 a IS A LIST OF a

DECLARE W2 AKA B2 IS A Duo OF B2, B2
DECLARE B2 a IS A LIST OF a

-- The same with a synonym that is its own alias and nothing else.
DECLARE W3 AKA B3 IS A B3
DECLARE B3 a IS A LIST OF a

GIVEN x IS A W1
GIVETH A W2
oops x MEANS x
